00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.510 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  steve kaufman um i'm going to do a few  videos here um yeah  i don't know how how much patience  people have to watch these things that  seem to  last a long time but i'm lazy i don't  want to write  so i'm going to speak here first of all  i went to the  convention of the american council of  teachers  of foreign languages in san diego uh  i must say i enjoyed it it was a  pleasure  to leave the rainy climate of vancouver  and go down to where the sun shines  i enjoyed san diego i got a real deal on  a hotel which turned out to be right  near the airport so it was about a 45 to  50 minute walk  in and back every day but i didn't mind  that i was  walking along the bay and it was very  pleasant  overall i was impressed with many things  at the  convention i was impressed by the  quality of the teachers that i met  many of them native speakers who spoke  the language that they were teaching  very well whether it was french or  japanese or chinese or spanish or  you know german or whatever  the seminars that i attended varied from  completely useless discussions about  you know research opportunities uh  presented by one professor  in heavily accented english he was  chinese he was talking about  different kinds of research that  demonstrated that  you know amongst other things that  advanced readers  read better than intermediate readers  and all kinds of useless junk  uh that was a downer uh the  there was some discussion about how  people introduced drama in their  classrooms and this kind of thing which  is okay it's not  my thing i don't really go for that i  just prefer  listening and reading  and there were some very interesting  discussions on new technology the ipod  you know it was a real revelation to me  to realize that there have been a  hundred thousand apps  applications apps written for the iphone  ipod touch  as opposed to 600 for blackberry and  60 for samsung or nokia or whatever  so there's this tremendous  uh outpouring of creativity  for this ipod iphone ipod touch  and even if you were to search the app  store looking for i don't know german  flash cards  you find lots like there's just more and  more applications being written for that  that have to do with language learning  there are a number of discussions  where university language teachers were  very frank  in stating that the era of the teacher  as the fount of knowledge and wisdom and  the instructor who's going to show you  how to do it  is already over and that the teacher  is there to direct traffic maybe to help  to uh coach to encourage to learn  because very often the students are much  more with it  in terms of the new technology than the  teacher  as an example i met a delightful teacher  of chinese a chinese lady  who has incorporated ipods and she  creates flash cards with  sound for learning characters for her  class and she  learned a lot about how to do it and how  to  follow up on what her students are doing  uh from her teenage daughters  so i think there's a lot of this going  to happen that the technology is  happening faster and the  the acceptance of the technology is  greater amongst young people  and the teachers are kind of out of it  and they're going to have to learn  there was one uh seminar on the ipod  and and the classroom and 80 people  showed up  but the presenter didn't show up and so  though  then a group of us stayed behind and  those of us who are using the ipod such  as we are at link  we started talking about what we did and  i was absolutely amazed  to hear the number of people who said  that they didn't know  what an ipod was well i am  quite convinced that at the next  conference of the a  c t f l or ftl whatever in boston  uh many many more people will know what  an ipod is  and how to use it i think that  technology is coming on us  very very fast and it's very convenient  there was an article that i quoted on my  blog  that in new york they found that kids  that were given ipods to listen to  a number of things in english including  songs that they were able to get  out of their special english as a second  language classroom  after one year whereas without that kind  of thing it was  six years and i have always felt this  and that's where  what we put into link get people to  listen to things that they enjoy  get them to put in the time to listen a  lot and they're going to learn  much faster than sitting in a classroom  with 25 other people  who don't speak the language well and  with the teacher who's trying to spoon  feed them  whatever the teacher thinks they should  be spoon-fed  so yes the ipod revolution is coming  and the combination of the ipod and the  internet  essentially makes language labs obsolete  you don't need them if you've got one  too bad  don't build one they're completely  unnecessary  they're they don't provide the range of  content  and the range of functionality that's  being developed for computer  app web apps ipod apps convenient take  it with you  you name it the language lab has is just  a white elephant the only advantage of a  language lab  is that it's something that the school  can show off to a prospective student  in the hope of enticing that student to  come there but if the student is at all  smart he realizes  that he doesn't want to sit in the  language lab if he can be doing most of  those things  on his ipod touch or  other mp3 playing device so i'm going to  stop there because i got a few other  ones  that i want to do thank you for  listening

